\title{Clique Sums and the Trace Calculus:\\
Exact Composition Laws for the Independence, Clique\\ and Chromatic Numbers}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{clique sum} glues two graphs $G_1$ and $G_2$ along a shared clique $K$ of size $k$.
We give a complete and sharp account of how the independence number $\alpha$, the clique
number $\omega$ and the chromatic number $\chi$ behave under this operation, distinguishing
rigorously between two notions that are routinely conflated: the \emph{strong} clique sum,
in which $K$ is required to be a clique of \emph{each summand}, and the \emph{weak} clique
sum, in which $K$ is only required to be a clique of the \emph{union}, its edges being
allowed to be shared out between the two sides.

For strong clique sums we prove: an independent set meets a clique in at most one vertex,
whence the sharp uniform bound $\alpha_1+\alpha_2 \le \alpha(G)+\min(k,2)$, with all three
regimes $k=0$, $k=1$, $k\ge 2$ attained; the folklore inequality
$\alpha_1+\alpha_2 \le \alpha(G)+1$ is \emph{false} for every $k \ge 2$, the minimal
counterexample being the path on four vertices; the exact decomposition
$\alpha(G) = \max_{T\subseteq K, |T|\le 1}(\alpha_1(T)+\alpha_2(T)-|T|)$, where
$\alpha_i(T)$ is the largest independent set of side $i$ whose intersection with $K$ is
exactly $T$; $\omega(G)=\max(\omega_1,\omega_2)$, every clique of a clique sum lying in a
single side; $\chi(G)=\max(\chi_1,\chi_2)$, by a colour-permutation gluing argument, and
consequently the preservation of $\chi=\omega$; and finally that the side condition
``the number of colours $n$ on a side is at least the weld size $k$'' is \emph{automatic}.

For weak clique sums all of this collapses: a three-vertex witness --- the triangle, split
as one edge plus a path --- satisfies $\chi(G)=3>2=\max(\chi_1,\chi_2)$ and
$\alpha_1+\alpha_2 = 4 > 3 = \alpha(G)+2$. Since $n \ge k$ holds automatically in the strong
setting and fails in this witness, the boundary between the two regimes is exactly $n\ge k$.
The unifying structure is a \emph{trace calculus}: an invariant composes across a separator
precisely when it can be relativised to the trace of its witness on the separator.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} clique sum, independence number, chromatic number, clique
number, graph gluing, tree decomposition, perfect graphs, trace decomposition.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

Structural graph theory is largely the study of \emph{decompositions}: statements of the
form ``every graph in the class $\mathcal C$ can be assembled from simple pieces by a
controlled gluing operation''. The most important such operation is the clique sum. Chordal
graphs are exactly the graphs obtainable from complete graphs by repeated clique sums;
series--parallel graphs, $K_5$-minor-free graphs (by Wagner's theorem) and, in the far
reaches, the minor-closed families described by the graph minor structure theorem all admit
clique-sum decompositions. Algorithmically, a tree decomposition of width $w$ is precisely a
recipe for assembling a graph by clique sums along separators of size at most $w+1$, and
essentially every ``linear time on bounded treewidth'' result is a dynamic program over such
an assembly.

The value of a decomposition is proportional to the number of invariants it \emph{composes}.
This paper determines, sharply, the composition laws of $\alpha$, $\omega$ and $\chi$ under
clique sums.

\subsection{A folklore inequality that is false}

The following argument appears, in various informal guises, whenever independence numbers
meet clique sums. Let $A_1$ be a maximum independent set of the left side and $A_2$ one of
the right side. Since $K$ is a clique, each meets $K$ in at most one vertex; their union
therefore over-counts at most one vertex, giving
\begin{equation}\label{eq:folklore}
  \alpha(G) \;\ge\; \alpha_1 + \alpha_2 - 1 .
\end{equation}
The argument is invalid: $A_1\cap K$ and $A_2 \cap K$ may be \emph{distinct} singletons, in
which case $A_1\cup A_2$ contains two adjacent vertices of $K$ and is not independent at
all. We show in Section~\ref{sec:sharp} that \eqref{eq:folklore} is not merely unproven but
false, already for $k=2$ and $|V|=4$, and that the correct universal constant is $\min(k,2)$.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item Precise definitions of strong and weak clique sums on a common vertex set.
  \item The One-Point Trace lemma and the gluing lemma for matching traces.
  \item The sharp uniform bound $\alpha_1+\alpha_2 \le \alpha(G)+\min(k,2)$ and the
        refutation of \eqref{eq:folklore}.
  \item The exact trace decomposition of $\alpha(G)$.
  \item Composition of $\omega$ and $\chi$, the automatic inequality $k\le n$, and
        preservation of $\chi=\omega$.
  \item Complete failure of both theories for weak clique sums.
  \item Algorithms extracted from the trace decomposition, with complexity analysis.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $V$ is a finite set and all graphs are simple and undirected with vertex set
$V$. We write $a \sim_H b$ for adjacency in $H$, and $G_1 \cup G_2$ for the graph on $V$
whose edges are those of $G_1$ together with those of $G_2$. A set $C\subseteq V$ is a
\emph{clique} of $H$ if any two distinct members are adjacent; $A \subseteq V$ is
\emph{independent} if no two of its members are adjacent.

\begin{definition}[Weak clique sum]\label{def:weak}
A graph $G$ is a \emph{weak clique sum} of $G_1$ and $G_2$ along $K$ with sides
$s,t\subseteq V$ if
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
  \item $G = G_1 \cup G_2$;
  \item $s \cup t = V$ and $s \cap t = K$;
  \item every edge of $G_1$ has both endpoints in $s$, and every edge of $G_2$ has both
        endpoints in $t$;
  \item $K$ is a clique of $G$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Clique sum]\label{def:strong}
A graph $G$ is a \emph{(strong) clique sum} of $G_1$ and $G_2$ along $K$ with sides $s,t$ if
(i)--(iii) of Definition~\ref{def:weak} hold and, in place of (iv), the stronger condition
\[
  \text{(iv$'$)}\qquad K \text{ is a clique of } G_1 \text{ \emph{and} a clique of } G_2 .
\]
We write $k=|K|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
Condition (iv$'$) implies (iv), so every clique sum is a weak clique sum. The converse fails
badly: in Section~\ref{sec:weak}, $K$ is a triangle of $G$ whose three edges are split
between the sides, so that $K$ is a clique of neither summand.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Why a shared ambient vertex set]
Presenting both summands on the same vertex set avoids the bookkeeping of an amalgamated
pushout while losing nothing: the private part of side~$1$ is $s\setminus K$, that of
side~$2$ is $t \setminus K$, and (iii) forbids either graph from having edges outside its
own side. In particular $G_1$ is edgeless outside $s$, so its independence number
\emph{restricted to $s$} is the meaningful quantity.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[Restricted invariants]
For a graph $H$ and $u \subseteq V$ put
\[
  \alphaof{H}{u} = \max\{|A| : A \subseteq u,\ A\text{ independent in } H\},\qquad
  \omega(H;u) = \max\{|C| : C \subseteq u,\ C \text{ a clique of } H\}.
\]
We abbreviate $\alpha_1 = \alphaof{G_1}{s}$, $\alpha_2 = \alphaof{G_2}{t}$,
$\alpha(G) = \alphaof{G}{V}$, and similarly $\omega_1,\omega_2,\omega(G)$. The chromatic
number $\chi(H)$ is the least $n$ for which $H$ admits a proper $n$-colouring.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Trace, traced independence number]
For $A\subseteq V$ the \emph{trace} of $A$ on $K$ is $A \cap K$. For $T \subseteq K$,
\[
  \alpha_1(T) = \max\{|A| : A\subseteq s,\ A \text{ independent in } G_1,\ A\cap K = T\},
\]
and $\alpha_2(T)$ symmetrically. A trace $T$ is \emph{admissible} if $T \subseteq K$ and
$|T| \le 1$; for admissible $T$ the maximum is over a nonempty family, since $T$ itself is a
candidate (a set of size at most one is independent, there being no loops).
\end{definition}

\section{The two structural lemmas}

\begin{lemma}[One-Point Trace]\label{lem:onepoint}
If $A$ is independent in $H$ and $K$ is a clique of $H$, then $|A \cap K| \le 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $a,b \in A\cap K$ with $a \ne b$, then $a \sim_H b$ because $K$ is a clique, and
$a \not\sim_H b$ because $A$ is independent.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Gluing lemma]\label{lem:glue}
Let $G$ be a weak clique sum of $G_1,G_2$ along $K$ with sides $s,t$. Let $A_1 \subseteq s$
be independent in $G_1$, let $A_2 \subseteq t$ be independent in $G_2$, and suppose
$A_1 \cap K = A_2 \cap K$. Then $A_1 \cup A_2$ is independent in $G$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
First, $x \in s\cap t \Rightarrow x\in K$, and the trace hypothesis transfers weld
membership: if $x \in A_1 \cap K$ then $x \in A_2\cap K \subseteq A_2$, and symmetrically.

Let $a,b \in A_1\cup A_2$ with $a\sim_G b$. Since $G = G_1\cup G_2$, either $a\sim_{G_1}b$ or
$a \sim_{G_2} b$; treat the first case, the second being symmetric. Then $a,b\in s$ by
(iii). If both lie in $A_1$ we contradict independence of $A_1$. If, say,
$b \in A_2\setminus A_1$, then $b \in s\cap t = K$, so by the transfer $b \in A_1$, and we
are back in the previous case. Exhausting the four membership patterns yields a
contradiction in each.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Trace-free gluing]\label{cor:tracefree}
In the situation of Lemma~\ref{lem:glue} with arbitrary $A_1,A_2$, the set
$(A_1\setminus K)\cup(A_2\setminus K)$ is independent in $G$, and the two pieces are
disjoint.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Both sets have empty trace, so Lemma~\ref{lem:glue} applies; a common element would lie in
$s\cap t = K$ and has been deleted.
\end{proof}

\section{The sharp uniform bound for \texorpdfstring{$\alpha$}{alpha}}

\begin{theorem}[$-2$ bound]\label{thm:minus2}
For every clique sum $G$ of $G_1,G_2$ along $K$ we have
$\alpha_1 + \alpha_2 \le \alpha(G) + 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose maximum witnesses $A_1\subseteq s$ and $A_2 \subseteq t$. By
Corollary~\ref{cor:tracefree}, $U = (A_1\setminus K)\sqcup(A_2\setminus K)$ is independent
in $G$, so
\[
  \alpha(G) \ \ge\ |U| = \big(|A_1|-|A_1\cap K|\big)+\big(|A_2|-|A_2\cap K|\big).
\]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:onepoint} applied on each side --- this is where (iv$'$) is used, as we
need $K$ to be a clique of $G_1$ and of $G_2$ --- each $|A_i\cap K|\le 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$-1$ bound for small welds]\label{thm:minus1}
If $k \le 1$ then $\alpha_1+\alpha_2 \le \alpha(G)+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take maximum witnesses $A_1,A_2$. If $A_1\cap K = A_2\cap K$, Lemma~\ref{lem:glue} makes
$A_1\cup A_2$ independent and inclusion--exclusion gives
$\alpha(G) \ge |A_1|+|A_2|-|A_1\cap A_2| \ge \alpha_1+\alpha_2-1$, since
$A_1 \cap A_2 \subseteq s\cap t = K$ has at most one element. If the traces differ then, both
being subsets of a set of size at most one, one is empty, say $A_1\cap K=\varnothing$. Then
$A_1$ and $A_2\setminus K$ have equal (empty) traces and are disjoint, so
$\alpha(G) \ge |A_1| + |A_2| - |A_2\cap K| \ge \alpha_1+\alpha_2-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$k=0$: exact additivity]\label{thm:minus0}
If $K = \varnothing$ then $\alpha(G) = \alpha_1+\alpha_2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both traces are empty, so Lemma~\ref{lem:glue} glues the two maximum witnesses, which are
disjoint since $s\cap t=\varnothing$. Conversely any independent set of $G$ splits into its
parts in $s$ and in $t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp uniform gluing bound]\label{thm:uniform}
For every clique sum $G$ of $G_1,G_2$ along a clique $K$ of size $k$,
\[
  \alpha_1+\alpha_2 \ \le\ \alpha(G) + \min(k,2).
\]
\end{theorem}

\section{Sharpness and the refutation of the folklore bound}\label{sec:sharp}

\subsection{Witness A: the path on four vertices ($k=2$)}

Let $V = \{0,1,2,3\}$ and set $s=\{0,1,2\}$ with $G_1$ the graph with edges $\{0,1\}$ and
$\{1,2\}$; $t = \{0,1,3\}$ with $G_2$ the graph with edges $\{0,1\}$ and $\{0,3\}$; and
$K = s\cap t = \{0,1\}$. Then $s\cup t = V$, all edges of $G_1$ lie in $s$, all edges of
$G_2$ lie in $t$, and $K$ is an edge --- hence a $2$-clique --- of \emph{both} summands. So
this is a genuine clique sum with $k=2$, and $G$ is the path $2-1-0-3$. Enumeration gives
\[
  \alpha_1 = 2 \ (\{0,2\}), \qquad \alpha_2 = 2 \ (\{1,3\}), \qquad \alpha(G) = 2 \ (\{2,3\}).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Failure of the folklore bound]\label{thm:fail1}
The inequality $\alpha_1+\alpha_2 \le \alpha(G)+1$ is false for clique sums: Witness A has
$\alpha_1+\alpha_2 = 4 > 3 = \alpha(G)+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of the $-2$ bound]\label{thm:sharp2}
Witness A satisfies $\alpha_1+\alpha_2 = \alpha(G)+2$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:minus2} is
attained.
\end{theorem}

The diagnosis is exactly the failure of the folklore argument: the maximum independent sets
of the two sides use \emph{different} vertices of $K$ (the left one uses $0$, the right one
uses $1$), so Lemma~\ref{lem:glue} does not apply. In trace language,
$\alpha_1(\{0\})=2$, $\alpha_2(\{0\})=1$, $\alpha_1(\{1\})=1$, $\alpha_2(\{1\})=2$ and
$\alpha_1(\varnothing)=\alpha_2(\varnothing)=1$, so the formula of
Theorem~\ref{thm:trace} returns $\max(2,2,2)=2=\alpha(G)$.

\subsection{Witnesses C and D: the small-$k$ regimes}

\begin{example}[$k=1$]
Let $V=\{0,1,2\}$ with all three graphs edgeless, $s=\{0,1\}$, $t=\{0,2\}$, $K=\{0\}$. Then
$\alpha_1=\alpha_2=2$ and $\alpha(G)=3$, so $\alpha_1+\alpha_2 = \alpha(G)+1$:
Theorem~\ref{thm:minus1} is attained.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[$k=0$]
Let $V=\{0,1\}$ with all graphs edgeless, $s=\{0\}$, $t=\{1\}$, $K=\varnothing$. Then
$\alpha_1+\alpha_2 = 2 = \alpha(G)$: Theorem~\ref{thm:minus0} is attained.
\end{example}

Hence $k \mapsto \min(k,2)$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform} is the pointwise smallest correct
function.

\section{The exact independence number of a clique sum}

\begin{theorem}[Trace decomposition]\label{thm:trace}
Let $G$ be a clique sum of $G_1,G_2$ along $K$ with sides $s,t$. Then
\[
  \alpha(G) \;=\; \max_{\substack{T\subseteq K\\ |T|\le 1}}
  \Big(\alpha_1(T)+\alpha_2(T)-|T|\Big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\le$) Let $A$ be a maximum independent set of $G$ and put $T = A\cap K$; by
Lemma~\ref{lem:onepoint} $|T|\le 1$, so $T$ is admissible. Since $G_1\subseteq G$, the set
$A\cap s$ is independent in $G_1$, and $(A\cap s)\cap K = T$ because $K\subseteq s$; hence
$|A\cap s|\le\alpha_1(T)$, and symmetrically $|A\cap t| \le \alpha_2(T)$. Also
$(A\cap s)\cup(A\cap t) = A$ and $(A\cap s)\cap(A\cap t) = A\cap K = T$, so
inclusion--exclusion gives $|A| = |A\cap s|+|A\cap t|-|T| \le \alpha_1(T)+\alpha_2(T)-|T|$.

($\ge$) Fix an admissible $T$ and optimal traced witnesses $A_1,A_2$ with $A_i\cap K = T$.
Their traces agree, so $A_1\cup A_2$ is independent by Lemma~\ref{lem:glue}. Moreover
$A_1\cap A_2 = T$: a common element lies in $s\cap t = K$, hence in $A_1\cap K = T$; and
conversely $T \subseteq A_1\cap A_2$. Inclusion--exclusion gives
$\alpha(G) \ge \alpha_1(T)+\alpha_2(T)-|T|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Size of the state space]
The maximum ranges over at most $k+1$ traces: $\varnothing$ and the $k$ singletons. This is
exactly the message one side must send to the other, and it is minimal: Witness A shows the
untraced pair $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$ is insufficient.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
Theorem~\ref{thm:minus2} follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:trace}: take $T=\varnothing$ and note
that deleting the at most one weld vertex from a maximum witness of side $i$ gives
$\alpha_i(\varnothing) \ge \alpha_i - 1$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Cliques, colourings and perfection}

\begin{theorem}[Cliques do not cross]\label{thm:nocross}
Let $C$ be a clique of a clique sum $G$. Then $C\subseteq s$ or $C \subseteq t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Otherwise pick $a\in C\setminus s$ and $b \in C\setminus t$; then $a\ne b$, so $a\sim_G b$.
But every edge of $G$ lies inside $s$ or inside $t$, contradicting $a\notin s$ in the first
case and $b\notin t$ in the second.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Composition of $\omega$]\label{thm:omega}
$\omega(G) = \max(\omega_1,\omega_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\ge$) Both sides are subgraphs of $G$. ($\le$) Let $C$ be a maximum clique of $G$; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:nocross} assume $C\subseteq s$. For distinct $a,b\in C$ we have $a\sim_G b$,
so either $a\sim_{G_1}b$, or $a \sim_{G_2} b$, in which case $a,b\in t$ as well, hence
$a,b\in K$ and $a\sim_{G_1}b$ because $K$ is a clique of $G_1$. So $C$ is a clique of $G_1$
inside $s$, whence $|C|\le\omega_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Automatic $k\le n$]\label{thm:kn}
If a side of a clique sum is $n$-colourable then $k \le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A proper colouring $c$ of $G_1$ is injective on $K$, since distinct vertices of $K$ are
adjacent in $G_1$. Hence $k = |c(K)| \le n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Colour transfer]\label{thm:transfer}
If $G_1$ and $G_2$ are each $n$-colourable, so is the clique sum $G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $c_1,c_2$ be proper $n$-colourings; by the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:kn} both are
injective on $K$. Hence $c_2(v)\mapsto c_1(v)$ ($v\in K$) is a well-defined bijection
$c_2(K)\to c_1(K)$ between subsets of the palette of equal size $k$, and extends to a
permutation $\sigma$ of the $n$ colours (match the complements, of equal size $n-k$). Then
$\sigma\circ c_2$ is a proper colouring of $G_2$ agreeing with $c_1$ on $K$. Define
$c(v)=c_1(v)$ for $v\in s$ and $c(v) = \sigma(c_2(v))$ otherwise. If $a\sim_{G_1}b$ then
$a,b\in s$ and $c$ separates them by properness of $c_1$. If $a \sim_{G_2} b$ then $a,b\in t$,
and for every $x\in t$ we have $c(x)=\sigma(c_2(x))$ --- immediate if $x\notin s$, and if
$x\in s$ then $x\in K$ where $c_1(x)=\sigma(c_2(x))$ --- so $c$ separates them by
injectivity of $\sigma$ and properness of $c_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Composition of $\chi$]\label{thm:chi}
$\chi(G) = \max(\chi_1,\chi_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\ge$) Monotonicity of $\chi$ under subgraphs. ($\le$) Each side is
$\max(\chi_1,\chi_2)$-colourable, so Theorem~\ref{thm:transfer} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Preservation of $\chi=\omega$]\label{thm:perfect}
If $\chi_1=\omega_1$ and $\chi_2=\omega_2$ then $\chi(G)=\omega(G)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\chi(G)=\max(\chi_1,\chi_2)=\max(\omega_1,\omega_2)=\omega(G)$ by
Theorems~\ref{thm:chi} and \ref{thm:omega}.
\end{proof}

This is the numerical heart of the classical statement that clique sums of perfect graphs
are perfect: an induced subgraph of a clique sum is itself a clique sum of induced subgraphs
along a sub-clique of $K$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:perfect} applies hereditarily. In particular
it re-proves that chordal graphs are perfect.

\section{The boundary: weak clique sums fail completely}\label{sec:weak}

Let $V=\{0,1,2\}$ and $s=t=K=V$. Let $G_1$ have the single edge $\{0,1\}$ and let $G_2$ have
the edges $\{0,2\}$ and $\{1,2\}$. Then $G = G_1\cup G_2$ is the triangle and $K=V$ is a
$3$-clique of it: a weak clique sum with $k=3$. It is not a clique sum, since $K$ is a clique
of neither summand. Here $\chi_1=\chi_2=2$, $\alpha_1=\alpha_2=2$, while $\chi(G)=3$ and
$\alpha(G)=1$.

\begin{theorem}[Colouring fails]\label{thm:weakchi}
$\chi(G)=\max(\chi_1,\chi_2)$ is false for weak clique sums: here $3\ne 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Independence fails]\label{thm:weakalpha}
$\alpha_1+\alpha_2 \le \alpha(G)+2$ is false for weak clique sums: here $4>3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[The clique law fails too]
The same witness breaks Theorem~\ref{thm:omega}: $\omega_1=\omega_2=2$ while $\omega(G)=3$.
Theorem~\ref{thm:nocross} is the culprit --- in a weak clique sum a clique of $G$ need not lie
inside a single side, since the weld is a clique of the union that no summand realises.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[The numerical boundary]\label{thm:boundary}
In this witness both sides are $2$-colourable while $k=3$; that is, $n=2<3=k$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:kn} this is impossible for a genuine clique sum.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary} identifies the hinge. From the point of view of colourings, the
condition ``$K$ is a clique on each side'' is equivalent to the numerical condition $n \ge k$
for the number of colours $n$ used on a side; the strong hypothesis buys it for free, and
when it is lost --- as here, where the two sides conspire to hide a triangle by each showing
only part of it --- both composition laws are destroyed simultaneously. In a weak clique sum
the summands can jointly encode structure that neither of them individually sees.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Independence number by trace enumeration}

Given a clique-sum presentation $(G_1,G_2,s,t,K)$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
  \item enumerate the admissible traces $\mathcal T = \{\varnothing\}\cup\{\{v\}: v\in K\}$,
        of which there are $k+1$;
  \item for each $T\in\mathcal T$ compute $\alpha_1(T)$: if $T=\{v\}$, delete the closed
        neighbourhood of $v$ and the rest of $K$ from $s$, solve the unconstrained problem
        and add $1$; if $T=\varnothing$, delete all of $K$ from $s$ and solve the
        unconstrained problem;
  \item compute $\alpha_2(T)$ symmetrically;
  \item return $\max_{T\in\mathcal T}(\alpha_1(T)+\alpha_2(T)-|T|)$.
\end{enumerate}
Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:trace}. The cost is $2(k+1)$ calls to an
independence-number oracle on graphs no larger than the sides: brute force gives
$O\big(k(2^{n_1}n_1^2+2^{n_2}n_2^2)\big)$ for sides of $n_1,n_2$ vertices, exponentially
better than the $O(2^{n_1+n_2-k})$ of a direct attack for balanced sides, and polynomial
whenever the sides are themselves tractable.

\subsection{Iterated clique sums along a tree}

If $G$ is assembled from pieces by clique sums along a tree with separators of size at most
$w+1$, root the tree and fold: maintain at each node the vector $(\alpha(T))_T$ of traced
independence numbers of the processed subtree and combine a child into its parent by
\[
  \alpha^{\text{new}}(T) = \alpha^{\text{parent}}(T)
     + \max_{T'\ \text{compatible with}\ T}\big(\alpha^{\text{child}}(T')-|T'|\big).
\]
Because the state is a single optional vertex per separator, the table has size $O(w)$ per
edge rather than $2^{O(w)}$. For $\chi$ and $\omega$ the fold is a single maximum over the
pieces, by Theorems~\ref{thm:chi} and~\ref{thm:omega}.

\subsection{Certified detection of the weak/strong distinction}

Verifying condition (iv$'$) costs $O(k^2)$ adjacency queries per side; verifying only (iv)
costs $O(k^2)$ queries in the union. The gap between the two checks is the entire content of
Section~\ref{sec:weak}, so an implementation must perform the former. A useful sanity check
from Theorem~\ref{thm:kn}: if a side admits a proper colouring with fewer than $k$ colours,
the presentation is not a clique sum and no composition law may be applied.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Chordal graphs.} A graph is chordal iff it is obtainable from complete graphs by
repeated clique sums; Theorems~\ref{thm:omega} and~\ref{thm:chi} then give $\chi=\omega$ for
chordal graphs by induction, and the trace algorithm specialises to the classical
linear-time computation of $\alpha$ via a perfect elimination ordering.

\paragraph{Tree decompositions and inference.} The trace calculus explains why the state
space for independent-set dynamic programming on a separator $S$ collapses when $S$ is a
clique: instead of tracking which subset of $S$ the partial solution uses ($2^{|S|}$ states),
it suffices to track which single vertex, if any, it uses ($|S|+1$ states). The same
phenomenon underlies junction-tree message passing on models whose cliques are genuinely
complete.

\paragraph{Structural decomposition theorems.} Wagner's characterisation of $K_5$-minor-free
graphs and the graph minor structure theorem express classes as clique sums of basic pieces.
The results here say precisely which invariants can be read off the pieces: $\chi$ and
$\omega$ immediately, $\alpha$ only after refining each piece by its trace data.

\section{Discussion}

Three lessons emerge. First, sharpness is a statement about all regimes at once: the bound
$\alpha_1+\alpha_2\le\alpha(G)+\min(k,2)$ is attained for each of $k=0,1,\ge2$, and a single
extremal example would not have settled the shape of the correct constant. Second, the
failure of a folklore argument is usually a missing hypothesis rather than a missing idea:
the classical derivation of \eqref{eq:folklore} is one clause short --- it needs the two
optimisers to have equal traces --- and making that clause explicit both refutes
\eqref{eq:folklore} and produces an exact identity in its place. Third, the right invariant
to compose is a relativised one: $\alpha_i$ does not compose, but $T\mapsto\alpha_i(T)$ does.
This is the trace calculus, and the pattern recurs: $\omega$ composes because cliques never
cross a separator, $\chi$ composes because colourings can be relabelled to agree on the
separator, and $\alpha$ composes only after refinement.

The weak/strong boundary deserves emphasis. There is a temptation to define a clique sum as
``glue two graphs so that the shared part is a clique'', which is ambiguous about \emph{whose}
clique it is. The three-vertex witness of Section~\ref{sec:weak} shows the two readings are
not merely formally different but mathematically opposite.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Iterated clique sums and a tree-decomposition trace calculus.} The trace formula
is a fold over a tree of clique sums: $\alpha$ of a graph assembled by repeated clique sums
along a tree should be computable by a dynamic program whose state is exactly the
$\le 1$-element trace on each separator, yielding a linear-time $\alpha$ for bounded-width
chordal-like assemblies with a provably minimal state space.

\paragraph{Perfection is preserved by clique sums.} The identity $\chi=\omega$ was preserved
numerically here, and the colour-permutation argument applies verbatim to every induced
subgraph of a clique sum; what remains is hereditary bookkeeping, giving a complete proof of
a classical ingredient of the toolkit surrounding the Strong Perfect Graph Theorem.

\paragraph{Fractional relaxations and the threshold $k=2$.} The integral obstruction in
Witness A --- a maximum independent set can be forced to use a weld vertex whose neighbours
on the other side are free --- is exactly what the fractional independence number $\alpha_f$
smooths out. We conjecture $\alpha_f(G)\ge\alpha_f(G_1)+\alpha_f(G_2)-1$ for all clique sums,
i.e.\ that the fractional relaxation obeys the folklore bound that the integral invariant
violates, and that the integrality gap of the gluing step is exactly one.

\paragraph{Weighted and multi-way sums.} Both the trace decomposition and the colour-transfer
argument should extend to vertex-weighted independence and to simultaneous sums of $m$ graphs
along a common clique, with the loss term becoming $\min(k,m)$ and the trace state space
remaining of size $k+1$.

\paragraph{Other invariants.} Which further invariants admit a trace calculus? Natural
candidates are the domination number, the fractional chromatic number and the Lov\'asz theta
function; each requires identifying what a witness carries across a clique separator, and the
size of that state space is precisely the algorithmic price of decomposition.

